WHAT EACH INSTALL PROFILE IS CLEARED FOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                                     core        [hwp]
  Author / edit / structural checks  yes         yes
  Layout render pass in verify()     yes (built-in engine)  yes
  HTML preview / lint / PNG / PDF    yes (PNG/PDF need local Chrome)  yes
  Old-binary .hwp conversion         guided exit  yes
  to_hwp read-back verification      degraded    yes
  Heaviest licence in the tree       Apache-2.0  PolyForm NC + AGPL
  Host it as a service               yes         NO
  Ship it in a commercial product    yes         NO
  Open-source it permissively        yes         NO

Previews, PDF export, and the verify() render pass are served by the
built-in layout engine (hwpxkit/preview.py, stdlib + Apache-2.0 only). The
former [preview] extra and the rhwp cache-replay renderer it powered were
removed once the engine surpassed them. The split is enforced, not advisory:
only two modules touch the restricted package -- hwpxkit/convert.py and
hwpxkit/hwp_export.py
(the .hwp read-back verification and the Hangul-2010 HTML fallback) -- and
every one imports them lazily, inside functions. In the core profile they are
never loaded, linked, or distributed. hwp_export's preferred jar route drives
vsdn/hwpConverter (Apache-2.0) as an external java process; nothing restricted
is involved in the conversion itself. Check it:

    python -c "import hwpxkit, sys; print([m for m in sys.modules if 'pyhwpx' in m])"
    # core profile prints []

